FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:32.657
So since you've both worked
with Go and the Go team

00:05:32.657 --> 00:05:34.990
for quite a while, could you
tell us a little bit about,

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.370
for the people that don't
know what Go is, what is Go

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.690
and why is it so
important and amazing?

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:45.110
ANDREW GERRAND: Well, so Go
is a programming language that

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:46.740
was developed here at Google.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.784
And we built it as a
solution to problems

00:05:50.784 --> 00:05:51.950
that we have here at Google.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:55.070
So it's designed
with building sort

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.610
of distributed systems in mind.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:00.270
And so this is really
important in the cloud space

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.730
because a lot of
the design decisions

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.540
we made in designing Go
for use inside Google

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.600
are also really
applicable to people

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.580
who build cloud software.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.420
One of the most sort of
prominent and obvious of those

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.970
is Go's support for concurrency.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:21.290
It provides a lightweight
threading model,

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.090
based around the
notion of Go routines.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.780
And then it provides
some other things

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.450
for synchronizing
and communicating

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.860
between those Go routines
and lightweight threads.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.060
And using those
concurrency parameters,

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.740
you can build really
nice concurrent software.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.460
And pretty much
most of the software

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:41.670
that people build
in the cloud space

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.600
is concurrent in some way.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.340
And so Go is a really
nice natural fit

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:51.130
for people who want to
build cloud software.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:54.900
And it's nice because
it's quite approachable.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.490
If you've used any language
which has a C-like syntax,

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.100
you'll understand vaguely what
Go code does if you see it

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.530
for the first time.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.370
And so it's very easy
for people to spin up on.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.720
And we've seen a lot
of our cloud customers

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.440
choose to use Go not
knowing anything about Go

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:14.660
and then have a really
good time doing it.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.740
And that seems to be a story
that we hear over and over

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:22.100
again is that people, teams,
will try writing some Go stuff

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.160
and then they sort of
fall in love with it.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.243
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Definitely
matches my experience.

00:07:26.243 --> 00:07:29.140
I started using Go well before
I joined the Go team, just

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.650
as my choice for App Engine.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:35.150
And I was surprised how
quickly I was super productive

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:38.415
and could write moderately
correct concurrent programs

00:07:38.415 --> 00:07:40.179
with-- compared to--

00:07:40.179 --> 00:07:43.284
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Moderately
correct, that is great.

00:07:43.284 --> 00:07:44.950
Could you just tell
us a little bit more

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:49.610
about your experience
writing Go on App Engine.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.270
Have you ran something big
that you could talk about?

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:53.910
CHRIS BROADFOOT:
Yes, so I worked

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:57.140
for many consecutive years
on the Santa Tracker project.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.640
I don't know how many people
listening are familiar with it.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.290
But basically every Christmas,
a bunch of engineers from Google

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:07.100
get together and write a fun
web app that tracks Santa

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.500
as he flies around the world.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.140
And at least for the
last three or four years,

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:14.250
the back end has
run on App Engine.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:18.070
And writing services
was relatively easy.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:19.950
Obviously with something
at a large scale,

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.690
you want to do things
like load testing.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.110
But it was relatively
straightforward to get this app

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:29.990
to scale to a very
large number of queries.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:33.090
Obviously you want to write your
app with your particular use

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:34.039
case in mind.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:38.110
And so, in this case, we had
a lot of very similar queries,

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:41.360
so things like
caching, using memcache

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.270
and Datastore were
very simple to do.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.530
And yeah, we didn't have any
production problems really.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:53.430
And App Engine scaled
to about 50 instances

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:57.200
to serve many thousands
of queries per second.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.540
And the throughput
from Go was very good,

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.850
many hundreds of qps
per App Engine instance.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:06.170
And they're not particularly
big instances, really.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.200
ANDREW GERRAND: And
it's nice that since he

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.880
wrote that in the first place,
the actual Go runtime has

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.240
seen a huge overhaul.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.060
And it's now
actually a lot faster

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:17.090
and can handle a lot
more concurrent load

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:17.842
than it used to.

00:09:17.842 --> 00:09:19.550
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Yeah,
that's true, yeah.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:22.700
It's been a while since I
ran load tests against it.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.940
And improvements
to Go 1.6 probably

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:28.275
would make a significant
difference as well.

00:09:28.275 --> 00:09:29.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.560
There's another project
that I also like a lot.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.020
If I remember correctly,
there was a Doodle

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:36.270
that was powered by Go.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.044
ANDREW GERRAND: Oh, yeah.

00:09:38.044 --> 00:09:39.460
I guess, a few
years ago now there

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:43.040
was a Google Doodle which
was for Thanksgiving where

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:48.740
you could like tweak the
turkey to-- there was a turkey

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.260
illustration, and
you could change

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.000
the feathers of the
turkey and its clothes

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.130
and stuff like that and a hat.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:01.714
And it was kind of ridiculous,
but people loved it.

00:10:01.714 --> 00:10:03.130
And you could hit
a random button,

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.190
and it would be
randomly composed

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.330
of these various things.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.420
So when you had a customized
turkey, you could share it.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:09.920
And

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.510
When you shared it, it had
to be shared as an image.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:17.644
And so most of that Doodle took
place in JavaScript in the app.

00:10:17.644 --> 00:10:19.060
But then when you
did a share, you

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.750
needed to actually share
like a JPEG of the turkey.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:27.570
So the Go app would
compose the turkey image

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.191
based on the various
layers, overlays.

00:10:30.191 --> 00:10:31.690
So it just did some
image processing

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:35.859
and then spat out
a generated JPEG.

00:10:35.859 --> 00:10:38.400
And when you launch anything on
the front page of Google.com,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.780
it kind of takes a
huge amount of load.

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:48.610
And Go and App Engine scaled
gracefully to meet the demand.

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:52.750
And we actually had a member
of the Doodle team, who'd

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:56.900
never used Go before,
wrote the app from scratch.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:58.510
I won't say it was perfect.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.280
I had a few review comments.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:05.940
But it performed well,
and it performed reliably

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.110
and did the job.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:10.110
CHRIS BROADFOOT: And I
think it's still running.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.980
Things on App
Engine, generally you

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.400
can deploy it and
still have it running.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:19.340
I visited an app I deployed
maybe three or four years ago,

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:20.637
and it was still working.

00:11:20.637 --> 00:11:22.970
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah, I mean,
I don't know if they still

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.470
enable the share and stuff.

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:28.280
I don't know how they archive
the Doodles, in retrospect.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:33.030
But it was certainly
nice for me because I'd

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:36.410
been working on the original
launch of Go and App Engine.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:39.610
And you know, this was kind
of like the first person that

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.950
came along and said, you know,
I want to use it for something.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:47.090
And it worked as
we expected it to.

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:49.090
You sort of spend all
this time building things,

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.631
and it's not until you actually
see it in the hands of a user

00:11:51.631 --> 00:11:55.020
that you really know that
you made the right choices.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I
just wanted to show,

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:58.310
it actually still works.

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:00.580
Even the Share
button still works.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:01.560
CHRIS BROADFOOT: We
should link to it in the--

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we'll
link to it on the show notes.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:04.140
MARK MANDEL: Very nice.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:05.770
ANDREW GERRAND: Maybe
people will be tweaking--

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:06.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Go
for power turkeys.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:08.929
ANDREW GERRAND: After
listening to this podcast.

00:12:08.929 --> 00:12:11.330
MARK MANDEL: So we talk a
little about scale, obviously,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:12.430
in terms of load and whatnot.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.513
But I think one of the
interesting things about Go

00:12:14.513 --> 00:12:16.922
particularly is kind of
like gofmt, which I think

00:12:16.922 --> 00:12:18.130
is the correct way to say it.

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:18.640
I know people say--

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:19.557
ANDREW GERRAND: Gofmt.

00:12:19.557 --> 00:12:20.514
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Gofmt.

00:12:20.514 --> 00:12:22.190
ANDREW GERRAND: It's
spelled G-O-F-M-T.

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:24.450
MARK MANDEL: No, I
can't deal with that.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:26.840
But I think it's
really interesting

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:29.257
like from the outset that
was like, this is the way

00:12:29.257 --> 00:12:29.840
to write code.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.964
This is the syntax format
that it needs to come in,

00:12:31.964 --> 00:12:34.460
and that lets you scale
in terms of teams.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.830
You want to talk a
little bit about that?

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:41.270
ANDREW GERRAND: One of my things
that I do in talks sometimes

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:43.380
is I say, raise your
hand if you've ever

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:46.100
had an argument with a
colleague about formatting,

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:46.980
like code formatting.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.490
And it's always the entire
room puts their hands up.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:51.360
And then it's like,
and keep your hand up

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.930
if you thought that was a
productive use of your time.

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:55.850
And all the hands go down.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.481
And I think everyone's
had this experience where

00:12:58.481 --> 00:13:00.480
one person likes it one
way and the other person

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.830
likes it another way.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.810
And there's really no like--
everyone has an argument,

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:07.120
but there's no really
objective truth

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.050
as to what the best styles are.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:11.670
And so gofmt just makes
those decisions for you.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.199
And you just have the canonical
Go style that everyone uses.

00:13:15.199 --> 00:13:16.740
And it's not anyone's
favorite style.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.270
It's not like the Go
team have this opinion

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:22.750
about brace position or tabs
versus spaces or whatever.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:28.075
We just made some
decisions, and that's it.

00:13:28.075 --> 00:13:29.700
Yeah, I mean, it's
a really nice thing.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.074
And we get a lot
of good feedback

00:13:31.074 --> 00:13:34.820
from people who have
engineering teams

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.740
or-- generally, the
longer somebody's

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:39.410
been in the software
industry, the more they

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:41.480
appreciate having
gofmt, I think.

00:13:45.191 --> 00:13:48.470
What motivated us was that at
Google, in other languages,

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:52.060
we have these very
meticulous style guides.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:53.840
And so a lot of
code reviews end up

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:58.580
becoming these sort
of really fixated

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.960
on the minutia of
the code, rather than

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:05.810
looking at the correctness
or the API design

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:07.690
or the things that are
really important when

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:08.800
writing software.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:10.600
CHRIS BROADFOOT: It's just
fewer things to think about.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:11.520
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:15.090
And there's actually
a really nice version

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.200
of gofmt called goimports,
which is functionally the same.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:22.395
It reformats code, but it
also will look at the names

00:14:22.395 --> 00:14:24.020
that you've used
inside the source code

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:25.500
and update your
import statements,

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:28.390
like the include or the
equivalent of include

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:29.015
in [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:29.015 --> 00:14:31.098
MARK MANDEL: This may be
a really stupid question.

00:14:31.098 --> 00:14:32.900
Does goimports do what
gofmt does as well?

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:33.200
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Yes.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:34.180
ANDREW GERRAND: Yes.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:36.270
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I always
use one after the other.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:37.010
It's redundant.

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.110
You don't need to.

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:42.140
CHRIS BROADFOOT: You'll never
see an import not used error

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:43.010
ever again.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:44.580
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah, yeah.

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:47.710
Or not found.

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:49.860
MARK MANDEL: No, I like
that sort of philosophy.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.637
And I think that also goes
to, I think when people start

00:14:52.637 --> 00:14:54.220
using Go sometimes
they sort of see it

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.889
as a fairly verbose language.

00:14:56.889 --> 00:14:59.180
But I think, to a degree
also, that's part of its power

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:00.840
in that you can
look at it and see

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.830
what is happening as each
step through the programming

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:04.640
language.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.142
There's no real magic there.

00:15:06.142 --> 00:15:07.350
What you see is what you get.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:08.974
And you can dig into
the core libraries

00:15:08.974 --> 00:15:10.740
and be like, OK, what
is this thing doing?

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.590
And you're not like, oh,
there's like some special flag

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:17.300
that this magic compiler has
that only the special libraries

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:17.810
get to use.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:19.380
It's all pretty standard stuff.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.230
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Yeah,
I think that's also

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:25.890
indicative of a greater trend
in the software industry too.

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:28.150
Like the last time I
used Java was maybe

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:29.990
three or four years
ago, and I think

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:33.430
Java development has changed
significantly since then.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:35.200
Although the language
has also changed,

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:36.980
I don't think that
the way people

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.980
approach programming is simply
because of language changes.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:44.500
It's because people don't want
to spend time figuring out

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:46.130
why their application
behavior changes

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:50.690
because of configuration or some
random file that's unrelated.

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:53.990
And I think the
simplicity of Go kind of

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.250
reflects a change
in the way people

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.090
are approaching
things in general.

00:15:58.090 --> 00:15:59.840
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah,
I mean, I don't even

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:02.026
think-- like Java
cops a lot of crap

00:16:02.026 --> 00:16:06.920
for being factories of
factories of factories

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:08.360
and all that kind of stuff.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:10.980
CHRIS BROADFOOT: But someone
typed the word factory, right,

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:11.660
to create those.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:12.996
It's not the language at all.

00:16:12.996 --> 00:16:14.370
ANDREW GERRAND:
And, you know, it

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.070
is possible to write
nice Java programs.

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:21.570
It's just the way it is used,
the culture that grew around it

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:26.650
in the sort of enterprise space
or whatever, is unfortunately

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:27.462
overly complicated.

00:16:27.462 --> 00:16:28.920
CHRIS BROADFOOT:
Yeah, and we don't

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.280
mean to disparage
just Java, I guess.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:34.846
ANDREW GERRAND: No, I mean,
it's true of JavaScript.

00:16:34.846 --> 00:16:37.112
It's true even in Python.

00:16:37.112 --> 00:16:38.820
There are lots and
lots of different ways

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:41.730
to do things in Python.

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:47.230
And just generally in
software as a whole, I think,

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:49.060
it sort of started off nice.

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:52.220
And then it just keeps
getting more and more complex.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:57.400
So Go's existence is kind of a
reaction to this sort of tide

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:58.300
of complexity.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.930
But I don't think Go is the
only project in the programming

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.930
landscape that's
focused on simplicity.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:07.170
I mean, predating Go is, say,
Rich Hickey with Clojure,

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.290
and he has a strong
emphasis on simplicity.

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:11.401
There's a great
talk that he gave,

00:17:11.401 --> 00:17:12.859
"Simplicity Made
Easy," which talks

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:14.272
about a lot of these things.

00:17:14.272 --> 00:17:15.730
The core of it is
that you actually

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.444
have to try really hard
to keep things simple.

00:17:17.444 --> 00:17:19.250
And I think complexity
is just something

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:23.339
that naturally arises in nature
as well as in human endeavors.

00:17:26.255 --> 00:17:27.839
You got to put the
brakes on a bit

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:33.830
sometimes to try hard to keep
things small and comprehensible

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.060
and so on.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:38.250
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Often you just
have to kind of redo and just

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:39.644
start again sometimes.

00:17:39.644 --> 00:17:41.810
Not necessarily talking
about programming languages,

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:43.800
but sometimes your
code, sometimes it's

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.790
just better to redo something.

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:49.420
MARK MANDEL: So since
we've covered a little bit

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:53.080
about the origins of Go and a
little bit of the philosophy

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.970
behind it, we could talk
now a little bit about,

00:17:55.970 --> 00:17:59.800
why do you think that Go is
so successful in the cloud

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:00.310
nowadays?

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:01.590
Like there's Docker.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:02.430
There's Kubernetes.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:05.220
There's so many--
basically any company

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:08.060
that has cloud somewhere in
the name, they're using Go.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:09.249
Why do you think this is?

00:18:09.249 --> 00:18:10.790
CHRIS BROADFOOT: I
think it goes back

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:13.740
to what we talked about at the
very beginning of the podcast

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:14.890
about concurrency.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:17.260
And I don't think we
mentioned networking ,

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:20.520
but I think Go is a fantastic
language for dealing with

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.690
networking, partly because
it's great for concurrency.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:27.490
But particularly writing
web applications,

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:32.360
I've found that it's-- just
the standard net/http package

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:36.610
and all of the libraries around
it create a really nice working

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:38.580
environment for creating
web applications.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:41.595
And I think everyone in the
community recognizes that too.

00:18:41.595 --> 00:18:43.970
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah, I think
it generally just gives you

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.220
the tools that you need to
write those kind of things.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:49.190
And also I guess one of
the reasons it makes sense

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:51.390
that Docker, for instance,
is written in Go,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.810
is that it spends a lot of
time talking to the operating

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:55.310
system.

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:58.430
And Go makes it really
easy to understand

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:03.400
the interface between Go
and the operating system.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:06.530
You can just walk through
the standard library

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:08.480
and see where the sys
calls are being made

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.740
and how that happens.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:14.510
And if you need to do more,
it's pretty straightforward

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:16.810
to understand how to
make the raw sys calls

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.850
or talk to lib C or
any of those things.

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:22.310
So it puts you a lot
closer to the system

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:24.630
than you would feel
in-- well, I guess,

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:28.670
any language that has like a
runtime, like Python or Java.

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:30.970
But if you're using C,
you're kind of using lib C,

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:35.790
and then lib C is a whole
other thing in itself.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:39.300
But also Go is a
memory safe language,

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:41.900
so you could be
using C, but then you

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:43.410
have to worry about
all the security

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:44.534
issues that come with that.

00:19:44.534 --> 00:19:47.890
And we continue to
see so many exploits.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:50.140
The memory safety of Go is
actually a really important

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:53.530
feature, and one that doesn't
get spoken about much,

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:58.540
simply because almost all modern
languages are memory safe now.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:01.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, as
a previous C++ programmer,

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:04.410
I know that one of my favorite
things of Go is the fact that

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:06.610
there's no memory corruption.

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:07.389
That is huge.

00:20:07.389 --> 00:20:08.680
CHRIS BROADFOOT: No seg faults.

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:10.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that
little thing that people

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:12.880
forget about, that's amazing.

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:13.760
So yeah.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:14.970
MARK MANDEL: Do you
also feel-- I mean,

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:16.820
Go is not the only language
that will combine down

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:17.653
to a single library.

00:20:17.653 --> 00:20:21.060
But it seems to be the reaction
against a lot of languages that

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:23.380
have runtimes that are bundled,
that ease of deployment

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:23.830
and that sort of stuff.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:26.220
Do you feel like that's
also been a big factor?

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.386
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah, a lot
of people talk about it.

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:34.450
On my Mac, I can build
a Linux AMD64 binary

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:39.760
and SCP it to a VM
somewhere, and it just works.

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:42.325
You don't need to worry
about any of the dependencies

00:20:42.325 --> 00:20:45.470
or bundling or
anything like that.

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:50.626
I think definitely
compared to any of the--

00:20:50.626 --> 00:20:51.710
what am I trying to say?

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:53.410
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:57.660
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah, like
Node or Python or even Java,

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:00.600
I mean, you still have to have
some story about bundling up

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:01.560
your deps.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:03.980
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Yeah, even
also the runtime version,

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:08.090
you end up with things like
rbenv and tools like that

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:10.510
to figure out what version
you're running under,

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.005
which is often very important.

00:21:12.005 --> 00:21:14.710
It's just a lot more code
to link against, I guess.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:17.210
And having them at
runtime, it also

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:20.792
just makes things a
little less predictable.

00:21:20.792 --> 00:21:22.750
ANDREW GERRAND: And I
guess in the cloud sense,

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:25.740
in a Docker container, you can
create a Docker container that

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:30.590
just has the Go binary in it
and nothing else and that's it.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:31.960
And that's kind of nice.

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:35.400
It's a very easy
thing to understand

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:39.354
in this world that's complicated
and difficult to understand.

00:21:39.354 --> 00:21:40.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:43.660
So for people that are
already convinced on using Go

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:46.790
and using Go in the cloud,
what are the different ways

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:49.200
that they can run Go on
Google Cloud Platform?

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.616
CHRIS BROADFOOT:
Yes, so there are

00:21:50.616 --> 00:21:53.940
many ways that vary
from very managed

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:57.010
to not being managed at all.

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:59.630
So some of my favorites
are Google App Engine.

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:02.770
I don't like messing with
deployment scripts and things

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:04.200
like that.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.241
I mostly write web applications.

00:22:06.241 --> 00:22:07.490
So they're a really great fit.

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:11.399
We have two sort of
flavors, I guess.

00:22:11.399 --> 00:22:13.190
So there's App Engine
standard environment.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:17.480
And this is what we used to
call a sandbox environment,

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:20.060
has quite a few
restrictions around how

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:22.090
you program your application.

00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:23.840
So you're not likely
to run an existing Go

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:28.130
application directly
on App Engine standard.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:31.130
But you can basically
do that with App Engine

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:34.710
flexible environment, which
has far fewer restrictions.

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:38.930
You have a main function
and a main package.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:42.010
So you basically run the
same application outside

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:43.800
of App Engine as you
do on your desktop

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.600
or on a different
cloud provider.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:51.370
We also have Kubernetes, which
is essentially an open source

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.890
version of the deployment
system we use at Google,

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:58.020
which is called Borg.

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:00.590
And this lets you
run your application

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:06.014
on many different nodes on any
compute provider very easily.

00:23:06.014 --> 00:23:07.430
There's also Compute
Engine, which

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:09.930
is basically just a bare VM.

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:13.310
And this means you have to run
your own deployment scripts

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:17.040
and startup scripts and
manage all of that yourself.

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:19.730
However, we do provide things
like kernel patches and things

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:21.620
like that on the fly.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.650
So it's still very different
to having a computer sitting

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:25.830
under your stairs, right.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:28.080
ANDREW GERRAND: Yeah, also
in the Compute Engine case,

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:31.640
you basically just get nothing,
in terms of scaling and so on.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:35.000
But then in the
App Engine cases,

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.350
either App Engine
standard or flex,

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:41.820
you can take advantage of the
order scaling infrastructure

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:42.380
that we have.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:46.580
So if your app gets a lot of
incoming requests suddenly,

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:48.920
a load spike, it
can quickly spin up

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:51.380
various instances, more
instances of your app,

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:55.510
and serve more request volume.

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:59.440
The key advantage that
standard has over flex

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:03.440
is that it can spin up
instances very, very quickly.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.370
The entire
infrastructure is built

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:13.650
around kind of keeping hot
spares of blank slate instances

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:17.950
that can then just basically
copy your app and launch it.

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:21.500
Whereas with flex,
each new instance

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.570
has to spin up a new VM
to serve that instance.

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.820
So if you can work within
the standard App Engine

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.930
environment, it's
actually a really nice way

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:29.680
to build software.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:32.780
It doesn't let you do things
like write to the file system.

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:37.350
And you need to use our APIs
to make like external network

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:39.106
requests and so on.

00:24:39.106 --> 00:24:40.730
But we've done our
best to kind of make

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:43.190
it feel really natural to
do that from inside Go.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:49.410
And generally if you use the
App Engine standard packages,

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:51.470
you will write an
app that will scale.

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:54.150
That's kind of the whole
premise of App Engine

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:59.140
is that we give you the APIs
to use that if you use them,

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:01.160
you won't write
an unscalable app.

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.410
I mean, it's definitely
still possible,

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:08.120
but the way it's designed kind
of makes it natural to do it.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.550
CHRIS BROADFOOT: It's
also great for small apps.

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:13.610
As I said before, I have
a multitude of toy apps

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:16.390
that continue running
without any oversight at all.

00:25:16.390 --> 00:25:19.140
And I think that's another-- a
real strength of the App Engine

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:20.330
platform.

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:21.590
It's very lightweight.

00:25:21.590 --> 00:25:23.381
ANDREW GERRAND: Oh,
and App Engine standard

00:25:23.381 --> 00:25:28.000
will run zero instances of
your app if no one is using it.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:29.890
So you don't even
pay for your app

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:31.490
when people aren't
using it, which

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.640
is a definite advantage
over the VM-based.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:36.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we
had a couple episodes ago

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:38.000
interviewed Humble Bundle.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:39.560
And they run the
whole infrastructure

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:41.476
basically on App Engine,
and they were talking

00:25:41.476 --> 00:25:42.880
about all of these benefits.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:47.390
If they get a huge spike in
traffic, they don't care.

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:48.600
Everything just works.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.350
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Yeah,
so I should mention

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.030
another thing
we're working on is

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:55.600
a set of packages and libraries
that sort of bridge these

00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:58.572
gaps so that if you start
with App Engine standard

00:25:58.572 --> 00:26:00.530
and need to do something
a little more special,

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:06.180
you can move to the flexible
environment, previously known

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:07.420
as Managed VMs.

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:11.700
And to sort of move between
these different deployment

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:14.490
modes and not have to
rewrite your application

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:19.240
because applications
should be agnostic of where

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:20.030
they're deployed.

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:21.613
You should just write
your application

00:26:21.613 --> 00:26:23.450
and be able to deploy
it wherever you like

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:25.040
and however you like.

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:29.930
So that's another focus and
real goal of the Google Cloud

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:30.872
Platform right now.

00:26:30.872 --> 00:26:32.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
