00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:38.080
But we have a really interesting
question of the week.

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:40.340
Francesc, would you
like to head that off?

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:42.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
so it's a question that

00:29:42.270 --> 00:29:47.045
comes to us through
the-- I forgot the name.

00:29:47.045 --> 00:29:48.170
MARK MANDEL: Slack channel.

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:50.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Through
the Slack channel, exactly.

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:53.440
We have a podcast Slack channel
in the Google Cloud Platform

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.400
community.

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:58.280
And the question
is, OK, so when do

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:01.010
you transform your data
in BigQuery via queries

00:30:01.010 --> 00:30:03.579
versus Dataflow for ETL?

00:30:03.579 --> 00:30:05.620
MARK MANDEL: It's an
interesting question, right,

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:08.810
because in theory you
could do it either way.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:11.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
so I think that the thing

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.680
to keep in mind here
is that BigQuery

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:18.330
is a very specific tool with a
very specific use case, which

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:20.090
is basically log analysis.

00:30:20.090 --> 00:30:21.480
It will do more than that.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:25.380
It will do, basically, analysis
of any kind of structured data

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:28.375
where the analysis that
you're trying to do

00:30:28.375 --> 00:30:32.760
matches pretty much what you
could consider a SQL query.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:36.640
And for those that don't
know about what ETL is,

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:39.510
basically ETL is a way
of processing data.

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:42.174
It's about extract,
transform, and load.

00:30:42.174 --> 00:30:43.590
So it's like you
extract the data,

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:45.550
you transform it in
some way, and then

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:48.340
you store it into
your data warehouse.

00:30:48.340 --> 00:30:51.810
And BigQuery is more
about once that data

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:55.080
has been loaded into
the data warehouse,

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:59.010
you can extract information
out of that data.

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:02.290
So if you're doing something
like, you have a lot of data,

00:31:02.290 --> 00:31:04.790
and you want to do an
average over all the values,

00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:07.090
or you want to find
some specific rows

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:09.290
and stuff like that,
that is basically

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:11.740
what BigQuery was built for.

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.007
But BigQuery doesn't
do everything.

00:31:14.007 --> 00:31:15.840
There's things like,
for instance, if you're

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:19.530
trying to do things with images
or really anything that is not

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:23.090
structured, not just
text, it's not really

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:25.030
going to work with BigQuery.

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:28.480
So in that case, you
should use Dataflow.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:31.960
And Dataflow, it's
basically-- if you have never

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:35.140
heard about Dataflow, but
you've heard about MapReduce,

00:31:35.140 --> 00:31:37.590
Dataflow is like
a generalization

00:31:37.590 --> 00:31:39.050
of what MapReduce does.

00:31:39.050 --> 00:31:42.384
So instead of having
just a-- so MapReduce--

00:31:42.384 --> 00:31:44.320
this is an interview question.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:47.194
How many phases
does MapReduce have?

00:31:47.194 --> 00:31:48.360
MARK MANDEL: How many faces?

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:48.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:51.450
MARK MANDEL: Like eyes, nose
and ears, and like, mouth?

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:52.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No, phases.

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:53.844
Making fun of my accent.

00:31:53.844 --> 00:31:56.010
MARK MANDEL: No, I didn't
understand you, I promise.

00:31:56.010 --> 00:31:58.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So how many
phases are there in MapReduce?

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:00.080
MARK MANDEL: That's a
great question, Francesc.

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:01.705
How many phases are
there in MapReduce?

00:32:01.705 --> 00:32:04.164
FRANCESC CAMPOY: One
could say map and reduce.

00:32:04.164 --> 00:32:06.995
No, there's map,
shuffle, and reduce.

00:32:06.995 --> 00:32:08.620
But you don't say
map, shuffle, reduce,

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:09.828
because it could be too long.

00:32:09.828 --> 00:32:12.980
But so basically, you
get those three phases,

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:14.520
and you make them composable.

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:16.275
So it's like a generalization.

00:32:16.275 --> 00:32:18.240
You can have as many
phases as you want.

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:22.040
So that allows you to
do whatever you want.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:24.480
So it's really powerful.

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:27.570
So if you want to do something
that matches the BigQuery use

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:29.790
case, go with BigQuery.

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:32.320
Otherwise, Dataflow
will be there for you.

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:35.040
And Dataflow is also very
good for a specific use

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.272
case, which is real-time
analysis and streaming.

00:32:37.272 --> 00:32:37.980
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:38.776
That makes sense.

00:32:38.776 --> 00:32:40.400
So if you want to
like window your data

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:42.280
or know exactly how
many times something's

00:32:42.280 --> 00:32:44.100
happened in the last half
hour or something like that.

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:45.141
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Exactly.

00:32:45.141 --> 00:32:47.950
There's also-- I've seen
some of them was basically

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:52.350
demonstrating how to
implement the Twitter--

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:53.505
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:32:53.505 --> 00:32:55.421
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The
hashtags around trending,

00:32:55.421 --> 00:32:58.060
that you can implement that with
Dataflow quite easily really.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:32:58.976
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:32:58.976 --> 00:32:59.476
Excellent.

00:32:59.476 --> 00:33:01.840
All right, so it sounds like
if I have structured data,

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:04.750
it fits in BigQuery, and
it hits that paradigm,

00:33:04.750 --> 00:33:06.150
I should just use BigQuery.

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:06.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Absolutely.

00:33:06.910 --> 00:33:09.120
MARK MANDEL: But if I sit sort
of outside of that paradigm,

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:10.390
then I might as
well use Dataflow

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:12.290
because it gives me more
flexibility in what I

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:12.720
need to do.

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:15.261
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, if you
can use BigQuery, use BigQuery.

00:33:15.261 --> 00:33:16.610
You will not regret it.

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:19.180
And if you cannot use
it, then use Dataflow,

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:21.890
and you can write
whatever you need.

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:23.160
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:26.086
Well, that sounds
like awesome stuff.

00:33:26.086 --> 00:33:27.710
But I think we're
